# tools/crypto_aead_witness.rish — the ChaCha20-Poly1305 AEAD (RFC 8439 §2.8) assembled in pure Rye from the two GREEN halves: Season G's
# fifth rung, Monocypher's authenticated encryption, proven against the RFC 8439 §2.8.2 known-answer and byte-for-byte against an
# independent implementation (Season G · Cryptography).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season G thread (Cryptography · Rye-native, Monocypher-parity, audit-ready).
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-175524_rye-first-crypto-parity-and-the-decision-wave.md (Season G names the ChaCha20-Poly1305 AEAD
# as the composition that unblocks the Lotus signed carry) and active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-180728_open-season-g-crypto-blake2b.md.
# Kin: crypto/chacha20.rye · crypto/poly1305.rye (the two halves this composes, GREEN) · crypto/blake2b.rye · crypto/sha512.rye. Monocypher
# (vendor/monocypher, CC0/BSD-dual) is the parity TARGET, never a copied line — our own composition, studied clean-room from the public RFC
# and Monocypher's public API (.claude/rules/gratitude-licenses.md).
#
# crypto/aead.rye composes ChaCha20 (confidentiality) and Poly1305 (the one-time tag) into RFC 8439 §2.8's authenticated-encryption
# construction: a one-time Poly1305 key grown from ChaCha20 block counter 0, the plaintext enciphered from counter 1, the tag over
# aad ‖ pad16(aad) ‖ ciphertext ‖ pad16(ciphertext) ‖ le64(aad_len) ‖ le64(cipher_len) fed block by block, and an open that recomputes the
# tag, compares it in constant time, and deciphers only on a match. The AEAD calls no std.crypto. Parity is proven at selftest against two
# references runnable now: the RFC 8439 §2.8.2 known-answer (the ciphertext AND tag for the RFC's own plaintext, aad, key, and nonce, hex
# for hex, opening round-trip), and Zig's own std.crypto ChaCha20Poly1305 as an independent implementation, byte-for-byte on both
# ciphertext and tag across the block edges and associated-data lengths of 0, 12, 16, and 40 bytes. A flipped tag, ciphertext, aad, or
# nonce each makes open refuse with AuthFailed and reveals no plaintext. The vendored Monocypher source is a held network fetch, so
# Monocypher-source parity is an honest horizon rung. Purely local — the key and nonce are the RFC's public vectors, no real secret, no
# signature (an AEAD authenticates under a shared key, it does not sign with an identity), no network, no funds, no real device.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/crypto_aead_witness.rish

say "crypto-aead: Language EN — Season G's fifth rung: the ChaCha20-Poly1305 AEAD (RFC 8439 §2.8) assembled in pure Rye from the two GREEN halves, ChaCha20 and Poly1305. This is the composition the decision wave named as the crux after the cipher and the authenticator each stood alone — Monocypher's authenticated encryption, the primitive Lotus's signed carry, Vault, and Comlink will reach for. It adds no new field math (the curve, edwards25519, keeps its own gated design round); it turns two proven stones into one usable seal, and it carries the published RFC 8439 §2.8.2 known-answer that ties parity to a neutral public reference."
say "crypto-aead: Style Radiant — the law is stated positively: grow a one-time Poly1305 key from ChaCha20 block 0, encipher the plaintext from block 1, authenticate the associated data and ciphertext with the padded MAC message, and open by recomputing the tag, comparing it in constant time, and deciphering only on a match — so the same key, nonce, aad, and plaintext always yield the same sealed pair anyone can replay against the RFC, and a single altered byte is caught before any plaintext is revealed."
say "crypto-aead: Lens TAME — the AEAD calls no std.crypto; it composes our own two primitives, bounds the message and the associated data at named ceilings, feeds Poly1305 block by block so no concatenation is materialized, and asserts its contracts on every function. Parity holds against the RFC 8439 §2.8.2 known-answer AND Zig's independent std.crypto ChaCha20Poly1305 byte-for-byte on both ciphertext and tag across the block edges and several aad lengths; a flipped tag, ciphertext, aad, or nonce each refuses with AuthFailed; malformed and over-long calls refuse before work begins. Monocypher-source parity is a named horizon behind the held vendor fetch. Purely local — the key and nonce are the RFC's public vectors, no real secret, no signature, no network, no funds, no device; constant-time timing-safety stays a separate named horizon."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "crypto/bin/aead"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p crypto/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build crypto/aead.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "crypto-aead: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "crypto/bin/aead.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "crypto-aead: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "crypto-aead: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN crypto-aead' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "crypto-aead: selftest did not print the GREEN crypto-aead line"
